  "title": "International Meridian Conference",
  "summary": "Delegates from many countries met in Washington DC in October 1884 to choose a common zero of longitude and basis for time-reckoning. Backed by the United States and Britain, a vote placed the prime meridian at Greenwich rather than Paris; the conference also took up a universal day and a dateline at 180 degrees.",
